  "knowledgePattern": "Apply a fixed-sum product bound twice and check simultaneous equality",
  "hints": [
    {
      "title": "Bound the product of b and c",
      "body": "<p>Apply the supplied inequality to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow></math>. Replace their sum using <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></math>.</p>",
      "recap": "The supplied inequality bounds a product using the sum of its factors.",
      "pitfall": "Keep the square on the whole average.",
      "pause": "Write a bound for a squared times bc.",
      "recall": []
    },
    {
      "title": "Apply the same inequality again",
      "body": "<p>Your bound contains the product <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. Apply the supplied inequality to these two positive factors.</p>",
      "recap": "The sum of these two factors is fixed.",
      "pitfall": "Both factors are positive because a, b and c are positive.",
      "pause": "Simplify the new upper bound.",
      "recall": []
    },
    {
      "title": "Check that the bound can happen",
      "body": "<p>Write the equality condition from each use of the inequality. Solve them together and check the original sum.</p>",
      "recap": "A largest value needs an allowed choice attaining the bound.",
      "pitfall": "Equality in only one of the two inequalities is not enough.",
      "pause": "Find a choice that attains your bound.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p2-b3-l06",
          "reminder": "A largest value needs an allowed choice attaining the bound."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "solution": "<p>The supplied inequality gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>b</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>a</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></math>. Therefore <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>b</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math>.</p><p>Apply the same inequality to the positive numbers <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></math>. Their sum is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>6</mn></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow></math>. Hence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>b</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>81</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></math>.</p><p>Equality in the first bound requires <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></math>, and equality in the second requires <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></math>. Taking <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> satisfies these and the original conditions. The maximum is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>81</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></math>, answer D.</p>"
}
